xvfb: share a system-memory screen with a capture client in place

A client that streams the screen otherwise has the server copy every frame
into its shared memory (XShmGetImage) and asks where it changed through
XDamage, a round trip each. Xvfb's own -shmem buffer does not serve it: a
RandR resize moves the screen into memory of its own and leaves the segment
holding the old picture, the segment is open to every local account, and it
carries neither where the screen changed nor whether a drawing operation is
under way.

- The screen of a server started without -shmem or -fbdir lives in a memfd,
  sealed against resizing, its pixels a page past a head carrying the
  geometry, a count of drawing operations (odd while one runs), and a
  retired flag; a resize moves the screen to a memfd of its new size and
  retires the old one.
- SELKIES-FRAMEBUFFER 1.0: QueryVersion; Watch(watch, drawable), which needs
  read access to the drawable as GetImage does and a local connection, and
  answers with the framebuffer read-only, the watch's memfd, and an
  eventfd; Unwatch(watch). A watch is a bitmap of the 16x16 tiles drawn
  into, which the server sets before each drawing operation on the screen
  pixmap and the client clears as it reads, and an armed flag, on which the
  server signals the eventfd once the operation ends. A retired framebuffer
  signals every watch. A screen whose pixmap moved to the GPU (glamor)
  answers BadMatch.
- Such a screen's cursor is never drawn into it, as a hardware cursor is
  not: GetImage hides a sprite drawn there, which a read in place would see.

